{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QCollatz","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QCollatz","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Basic","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.QIntegerCanonical","lean:module:QOU.QuantumObservableUniverse.CategoricalFoundations","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic"],"declarations":17,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.contact_threshold_lt_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.contact_threshold_lt_two","kind":"theorem","line":243,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L243-L269","doc":"**One-sided: `v* < 2`** (equivalently `[3] < [2]²`).\n\nThis docstring previously announced the two-sided `1 < v* < 2`, which the\nstatement and proof below never established. The lower half `1 < v*`\n(equivalently `[2] < [3]`) is proved in\n`QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzThreeForms.one_lt_contactThreshold`, and the\ntwo-sided form is `contactThreshold_mem_Ioo` there. The lower half is what\nrules out a *single* halving per tripling, so it is load-bearing for the\nclaim that the integer threshold is exactly two.\n\nSince v* < 2 is proved algebraically ([3] < [2]²), the integer\ncondition ⟨v⟩ ≥ 2 (two halvings per tripling) suffices — and\navoids transcendental quantities entirely. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.qInt_gap_3_8_fails","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.qInt_gap_3_8_fails","kind":"theorem","line":218,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L218-L237","doc":"The (3,8) gap is NOT always positive: `[3]²  - [8]` has\nnegative coefficients.  For large q, `[8] > [3]²`.\n\nThis means the (3,8) generalized Collatz does NOT have a\ncontraction gap for all q > 0 — unlike Collatz (2,3). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.qInt_gap_3_4","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.qInt_gap_3_4","kind":"theorem","line":205,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L205-L216","doc":"The (3,4) gap: `[3]² - [4] > 0` for all `q > 0`.\n(Trivial case: 9 ≫ 4 classically.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.qInt_coprime","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.qInt_coprime","kind":"theorem","line":165,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L165-L182","doc":"**Coprimality of [2]_q and [3]_q.**\n\nThe Skein divisibility identity `[3] = q·[2] + 1` is a\nBézout relation: `[3] - q·[2] = 1`, so `gcd([2], [3]) = 1`.\n\nConsequence: `[3]^a ≡ 1 (mod [2])` for all `a ≥ 0`, while\n`[2]^b ≡ 0 (mod [2])` for `b ≥ 1`.  These can never be equal,\nso the equation `[3]^a = [2]^b` has no solution for `a, b > 0`.\n\nThis proves: the Skein-Collatz map has **no periodic cycles**\nother than the fixed point P = 1.  A cycle with k_odd odd steps\nwould require `[3]^{k_odd} = [2]^k`, which is impossible. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.skein_convex_coeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.skein_convex_coeff","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L149-L159","doc":"The Skein convexity coefficient `q⁻²` is in `(0, 1)` for `q > 1`.\n\nThe Skein relation J(L₊) = q⁻²·J(L₋) + (1-q⁻²)·J(L₀)\nis a convex combination, so crossing resolution is non-expansive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.geometric_mean_contraction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.geometric_mean_contraction","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L139-L147","doc":"The geometric mean contraction per cycle: `√([3]_q/[2]_q²) < 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.contraction_ratio_lt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.contraction_ratio_lt_one","kind":"theorem","line":132,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L132-L137","doc":"The contraction ratio `[3]_q/[2]_q²` is strictly below 1. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.relative_margin","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.relative_margin","kind":"theorem","line":119,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L119-L126","doc":"The relative margin: `[2]_q² / [3]_q = 1 + q/[3]_q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.qInt3_lt_qInt2_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.qInt3_lt_qInt2_sq","kind":"theorem","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L114-L117","doc":"Direct consequence: `[3]_q < [2]_q²` for `q > 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.qInt_gap","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.qInt_gap","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L102-L112","doc":"**Contraction gap**: `[2]_q² - [3]_q = q`.\n\nTwo halvings (each dividing by [2]_q) more than compensate\none tripling (multiplying by [3]_q).  The margin between\n[2]_q² and [3]_q is exactly q — the substrate parameter.\n\nAt q = 1: 2² - 3 = 1.  At q₀ ≈ 1.1097: 2.11² - 3.34 ≈ 1.11. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.skein_divisibility","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.skein_divisibility","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L87-L96","doc":"**Skein divisibility**: `[3]_q = q · [2]_q + 1`.\n\nAt q = 1 this is 3 = 1·2 + 1.  In the Skein-Collatz map,\nthis identity ensures the odd branch is well-defined:\nfor q-odd P (meaning [2]_q ∣ P+1), the numerator\n[3]_q·P + 1 = q·[2]_q·P + (P+1) is divisible by [2]_q. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.qInt_three_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.qInt_three_pos","kind":"theorem","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L79-L81","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.qInt_two_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.qInt_two_pos","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L75-L77","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.qInt_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.qInt_three","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L68-L73","doc":"`[3]_q = q² + q + 1` for `q ≠ 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.qInt_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.qInt_two","kind":"theorem","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L61-L66","doc":"`[2]_q = q + 1` for `q ≠ 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.qInt_eq_canonical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.qInt_eq_canonical","kind":"theorem","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L49-L59","doc":"**Bridge to the canonical GEOMETRIC bracket** — note which one.\n\nThis site is `(qᵏ − 1)/(q − 1)`, the geometric form, *not* the balanced\n`[k]_q` that most of the corpus means.  Tying it to `qIntBal` would be a\nmis-consolidation, and the module's `qIntGeo` docstring says why: the geometric\nform is bar-*covariant* with weight `−(k−1)`, not bar-even, so it cannot carry a\nbar-even quantity.  The `q ≠ 1` hypothesis is `geom_sum_eq`'s and is real —\nat `q = 1` the closed form is `0/0 = 0` while the sum is `k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Substrate.qInt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Substrate.qInt","kind":"def","line":45,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QCollatz.lean#L45-L47","doc":"The q-integer `[k]_q = (qᵏ - 1)/(q - 1)` ","signatureChunk":0}]}